+
+    M(A) >= 2^(2theta-1) k_(1/(4C)) n^theta.
+
+This follows directly from the corrected prime range and K_B<=2M. If the cyclic theorem holds only for large q, finitely many smaller n can be absorbed using M(A)>=1/2. Separate pointwise constants c(a)>0 at every fixed a do not, without local uniformity or an explicit lower bound, imply a positive infimum over [1/(4C),1/2]. The conditional theorem here states the needed uniform quantifier explicitly.
+
+NUMERICAL experiment in progress. check_round14.py runs Gurobi K-minimization for all odd primes 5<=q<=61, at cardinalities 2*round(q/8) and 2*round(q/16), with at least one pair. It independently exhausts every symmetric set at those cardinalities for q<=31 using integer enclosures of Arb cosine coefficients and cyclotomic equality tests. Stored witnesses get exact rational Boolean-power checks and 220-bit Arb spectral/inequality checks. An initial strict-positivity assertion rejected the q=5 sharp Parseval equality; the verifier was corrected to require an exact cyclotomic two-level equality certificate in that case. This was a verifier boundary case, not a failed mathematical inequality.
